## Goal
See how the six samples in this project cluster: a principal component analysis of the
gene-level counts, with samples coloured by condition (untreated vs treated).

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design (native) | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
One Python script, `scripts/pca.py`, run in the `gars-bio` conda environment (pandas 3.0.5,
numpy 2.5.2, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1 -- all pinned in the lockfile; nothing is
installed). All steps run in a single batch job. Inputs are opened read-only; nothing is
written outside `results/`.

1. Resolve both inputs at run time with
   `python3 _system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --type counts_gene`
   and `--type design`; abort if either fails to resolve.
2. Read the count matrix (tab-separated); set `gene_id` as the index and drop `gene_name`.
   Read the design (CSV). Abort unless the count columns and the design's `sample_id` values
   are exactly the same set of six samples; order the columns to match the design.
3. Normalise for library size: counts per million (CPM = count / column sum x 1e6), then
   transform with log2(CPM + 1).
4. Drop genes with zero variance across the six samples. Keep the 500 most variable genes by
   variance of log2(CPM + 1) -- or all remaining genes if fewer than 500 (the matrix has 200).
5. Centre each gene to mean zero (no unit-variance scaling) and run
   `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes), svd_solver="full")`.
6. Write the per-sample scores (`sample_id`, `condition`, PC1..PCn) and the per-component
   explained variance (component, variance ratio, cumulative ratio).
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2 with matplotlib: one point per sample, coloured by `condition` with a
   legend, each point labelled with its `sample_id`, axis labels carrying the percentage of
   variance explained. Save as PNG at 300 dpi.

